### What is striped lock partitioning?

Striped locking divides a single global lock into N independent locks. Each key maps to a stripe by hashing, and only operations on the same stripe contend with each other.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class StripedLock {
    private final ReentrantLock[] locks;
    private final int mask;

    public StripedLock(int stripes) {
        stripes = Integer.highestOneBit(stripes - 1) << 1;  // round to power of 2
        locks = new ReentrantLock[stripes];
        for (int i = 0; i < stripes; i++) {
            locks[i] = new ReentrantLock();  // unfair (default, faster)
        }
        mask = stripes - 1;
    }

    public ReentrantLock getLock(Object key) {
        return locks[spread(key.hashCode()) & mask];
    }

    private static int spread(int h) {
        return h ^ (h >>> 16);  // mix high bits into low bits
    }
}
```

### How do you use it for per-account operations?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class StripedAccountService {
    private final StripedLock stripedLock = new StripedLock(16);
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> auditLog = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        ReentrantLock lock = stripedLock.getLock(accountId);
        lock.lock();
        try {
            BigDecimal balance = accounts.getOrDefault(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO);
            accounts.put(accountId, balance.subtract(amount));
            auditLog.put(accountId, "Debited " + amount);  // multi-structure atomic!
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does striped locking prevent cache stampede?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class CacheStampedeProtection {
    private final StripedLock cacheLock = new StripedLock(64);
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        BigDecimal cached = cache.get(accountId);
        if (cached != null) return cached;

        ReentrantLock lock = cacheLock.getLock(accountId);
        lock.lock();  // all threads for SAME key → same stripe → only 1 enters
        try {
            cached = cache.get(accountId);  // double-check after lock
            if (cached != null) return cached;
            BigDecimal loaded = loadFromDB(accountId);  // only ONE thread loads
            cache.put(accountId, loaded);
            return loaded;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal loadFromDB(String accountId) {
        return new BigDecimal("75000.00");
    }
}
```

### When should you use striped locks vs ConcurrentHashMap?

Use ConcurrentHashMap when operations are within a single map and atomicity is per-key (computeIfAbsent, merge). Use striped locks when you need atomicity across multiple data structures or when you need to prevent cache stampede for expensive loads.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How many stripes should you use? 16-64 is typical. More stripes reduce contention but increase memory. Profile under realistic load to find the right balance.
- Can two different keys hash to the same stripe? Yes. This is similar to hash collisions. They will contend for the same lock. With enough stripes, collisions are rare.
